-## Secrets for CI and deployment
+The backend serving its own frontend (step 4 above) is what makes a
+tunnel-shared link work at all: a browser won't let an https tunnel page
+call a plain http API, and a same-origin relative URL sidesteps that
+entirely.
 
-Store `GROQ_API_KEY` in your platform's secret manager rather than in source
-control. For GitHub Actions, add it as a repository secret and provide it to
-the runtime as `${{ secrets.GROQ_API_KEY }}`. Rotate a key promptly if it is
-ever exposed.
+1. In `.env`, set:
+   ```bash
+   DEMO_MODE="true"
+   ADMIN_TOKEN="pick-a-long-random-string"
+   ```
+   `DEMO_MODE` turns on every visitor-facing protection described below
+   (shared-course write protection, nothing else needs to change). With
+   no `ADMIN_TOKEN` set, `DEMO_MODE=true` simply locks shared-course
+   writes outright rather than leaving them open.
+2. Start the backend, then start your tunnel (Cloudflare Tunnel or ngrok)
+   pointed at port 8000, and share the tunnel's URL.
+3. To add or remove a shared course yourself while the link is public,
+   open the tunnel URL once with `?admin=<the ADMIN_TOKEN you set>`
+   appended — your browser stores it and strips it from the address bar
+   immediately, so the link you actually share never contains it. Every
+   other visitor's browser never has it, and the UI gives no visible sign
+   the mechanism exists.
+4. Watch the console: a startup line logs the running config (model, demo
+   mode, rate limits, TTL), and `GET /health` reports live uptime,
+   active session count, and how many Groq calls are in flight.
+
+## Production readiness (v1.4)
+
+Everything below is in `backend/config.py`, documented with its default
+in `.env.example` — none of it needs to change for local development.
+
+- **Rate limiting** — two windows per visitor (per-minute and per-day),
+  identified by the real client IP even behind a tunnel, enforced on
+  every endpoint that calls the embedder or the LLM (not just chat).
+- **Input/resource caps** — a per-message length cap; chat history
+  trimmed to a bounded number of recent turns; uploads read in bounded
+  chunks and rejected the moment they exceed the size cap, rather than
+  after being pulled fully into memory; a per-device notebook quota (doc
+  count and total size).
+- **Data lifecycle** — a background sweep auto-deletes chat/quiz sessions
+  and notebook uploads after a configurable number of idle hours; a
+  "Delete my data" link in the sidebar does the same immediately, on
+  request.
+- **Resilience** — a timeout on every Groq request; a process-wide cap on
+  how many Groq calls run at once, so the assistant degrades to a polite
+  "I'm handling a lot right now" under load instead of piling up or
+  hanging.
+- **Health + logging** — `GET /health`; one structured log line per
+  request with a short id (also returned as `X-Request-ID`); rate-limit
+  and admin-check rejections are logged (never the attempted token
+  itself).
 
